--- !u!114 &1241479726
MonoBehaviour:
  m_ObjectHideFlags: 0
  m_CorrespondingSourceObject: {fileID: 0}
  m_PrefabInstance: {fileID: 0}
  m_PrefabAsset: {fileID: 0}
  m_GameObject: {fileID: 1241479722}
  m_Enabled: 1
  m_EditorHideFlags: 0
  m_Script: {fileID: 11500000, guid: af6c11f0d531f4249801ea5c2de41bcb, type: 3}
  m_Name: 
  m_EditorClassIdentifier: Assembly-CSharp::ReadableTrigger
  interactionPrompt: Read
  backgroundImage: {fileID: 21300000, guid: 5face2da520a6fa4fa8eff1c614aff70, type: 3}
  fontOverride: {fileID: 11400000, guid: e4cf2c21c93fda745b09b91b791a22c2, type: 2}
  overrideAlignment: 0
  textAlignment: 257
  overrideMargins: 0
  textMargins: {x: 0, y: 0, z: 0, w: 0}
  overrideLineSpacing: 0
  lineSpacing: 0
  overrideFontSize: 1
  fontSize: 18
  overrideFontColor: 1
  fontColor: {r: 0, g: 0, b: 0, a: 1}
  pages:
  - "Excerpt from the Gravekeeper\u2019s Notes\nUnregistered visitors\n\nThere are
    two women who periodically arrive at the gates.\n\nThey never arrive together,
    though each insists the other has already been allowed through.\n\nBoth claim
    familiarity with the dead. Each knows names not written on stone. When asked
    how, one answers immediately and incorrectly; the other answers slowly and with
    details that do not improve clarity. I have learned that correcting either of
    them is a mistake.\n\nOne always asks about access. Locks, patrols, times when
    the soil is softest. She speaks of inheritance, of what is owed, of what should
    not be left unattended. When refused, she smiles as though refusal were expected.
    She returns anyway."
  - 'The other asks about boundaries. About what is held and what is trapped. She
    speaks of weight, pressure, the cost of keeping certain things in place. When
    refused, she looks surprised, then relieved, then disappointed. She does not
    argue. She simply adjusts her request the next time.


    I do not record which
    woman makes which request. It changes depending on who is listening.


    One
    of them is never permitted entry. This decision is simple and does not vary.
    She knows this and tests it regardless, offering reasons that contradict one
    another. Sometimes she claims permission already granted. Sometimes she claims
    necessity. Once, she claimed I had asked her to come.'
  - "The other is allowed in under specific circumstances. Weather, time, and recent
    burials all factor into this. I do not explain my reasoning to her. When turned
    away, she accepts it as provisional. When admitted, she does not linger.\n\nEach
    accuses the other of dishonesty. Each provides evidence. Both sets are convincing.
    Neither elaborate much.\n\nOne never fails to mention the other by implication.
    The other does not acknowledge their sister unless asked. \n\nThe one who is
    lying should never be let in."
  enableLowSanityOverride: 1
  lowSanityThreshold: 0.4
  lowSanityOverrides:
    overridePages: 1
    pages:
    - I should let her in
    overrideBackground: 0
    backgroundImage: {fileID: 0}
    fontOverride: {fileID: 11400000, guid: 759accaa4ac2581479aa673fda0ef5c9, type: 2}
    overrideAlignment: 0
    textAlignment: 257
    overrideMargins: 0
    textMargins: {x: 0, y: 0, z: 0, w: 0}
    overrideLineSpacing: 0
    lineSpacing: 0
    overrideFontSize: 1
    fontSize: 60
    overrideFontColor: 1
    fontColor: {r: 0.7924528, g: 0, b: 0, a: 1}
    enableShake: 1
    shakeIntensity: 3
    shakeSpeed: 25
